## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of CGO boundaries: safe callbacks from C to Go, cross-language memory ownership, and avoiding memory leaks in forwarding data.

## Key Concepts
- CGO callback safety: cannot pass Go function pointers to C; use a registry to bridge.
- Memory ownership: C allocates strings; Go copies and must free via free().
- Concurrency: multiple streams require isolated handles and proper channel closure.

## Code Example
```javascript
// C header (cgoback_strings.h)
void stream_strings(const char* prefix, int n, void (*cb)(char* msg, void* ctx), void* ctx);
```
```javascript
// Minimal C implementation (cgoback_strings.c)
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "cgoback_strings.h"

static void default_cb(char* msg, void* ctx); // bridge to Go via callback table

void stream_strings(const char* prefix, int n, void (*cb)(char* msg, void* ctx), void* ctx) {
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    char buf[256];
    snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "%s%d", prefix, i);
    char* s = (char*)malloc(strlen(buf) + 1);
    strcpy(s, buf);
    cb(s, ctx);
  }
}
```
```javascript
